{
  "teamId": 138,
  "year": 2021,
  "narrative": "The 2021 St. Louis Cardinals were a team that tested their fans' patience before rewarding their faith in spectacular fashion. Anchored by the first-year pairing of Nolan Arenado and Paul Goldschmidt, the Cardinals were a legitimate contender on paper from Opening Day, but the reality of the early season was messier. April and May brought winning records but inconsistent results, with moments of genuine promise undermined by shaky bullpen outings — particularly from closer Alex Reyes, who flashed electric stuff but also surrendered crushing home runs at the worst possible times.\n\nJune was the low point. A 10-17 collapse sent the Cardinals tumbling in the NL Central standings and raised serious questions about the team's ceiling. Reyes continued to struggle with runners on base, and the offense couldn't paper over the pitching cracks. July was steadier — a 12-11 record that kept the Cardinals afloat — but the team still felt like a underachiever relative to its talent level. The All-Star break came and went without any sense that a turnaround was imminent.\n\nThen came the September miracle. Posting an astonishing 22-7 record, the Cardinals caught fire behind an offensive explosion led by Tyler O'Neill, who hit some of the most dramatic home runs of the season. O'Neill's power surge, combined with a locked-in Goldschmidt and a rotation anchored by Adam Wainwright, propelled St. Louis from the fringes of the wild card race to clinching a postseason berth on the final days of the regular season. The final weekend brought white-knuckle drama against the Cubs, with the Cardinals needing every last win to hold off challengers.\n\nThe Wild Card game against the Los Angeles Dodgers proved to be a bridge too far. Adam Wainwright, the veteran warrior, carried St. Louis deep into the game, but in the end Chris Taylor's decisive home run off Alex Reyes extinguished the Cardinals' October hopes. It was a fitting if painful coda — Reyes, the electric closer who had both thrilled and frustrated all year, delivering the season's final heartbreak on baseball's biggest stage. Still, 90 wins and a postseason appearance validated a franchise in transition and laid the groundwork for what was to come.",
